From regetz at nceas.ucsb.edu  Thu Sep 17 03:11:23 2009
From: regetz at nceas.ucsb.edu (Jim Regetz)
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2009 18:11:23 -0700
Subject: [Phylobase-devl] unification of tree data slots
In-Reply-To: <4AB1873B.1050909@ufl.edu>
References: <4AB163DD.20701@nceas.ucsb.edu>	<1253148118.24186.10.camel@francois-laptop>
	<4AB1873B.1050909@ufl.edu>
Message-ID: <4AB18CBB.20204@nceas.ucsb.edu>

If truly you mean most trees will have tip data *only*, as opposed to 
tip data *mostly*, a solution is that we don't require every node to 
have a row in the data frame. So if you only supply tip data, then we'll 
only store rows for the tips, and for internal tips the NAs will be 
implied (but not stored) for all columns.

The caveat is that if you want even just a single column of data for 
nodes, then you'll actually have to store NAs for all the other columns 
as well.

So for a 3-tip (2-internal node) tree, if you only have tip data (but no 
node data) for columns a and b, then this is your space-efficient data 
frame:

@data
     a   b
1   x   x
2   x   x
3   x   x

But if you decide to add a column c for node data, then yes, we'd need 
to expand it to this:

@data:
     a   b   c
1   x   x  NA
2   x   x  NA
3   x   x  NA
4  NA  NA   x
5  NA  NA   x

Does that help at all? I guess I don't really blink much at the idea of 
"doubling the amount of data storage", but if others have very practical 
reasons for avoiding this, I understand.

BTW, I think the phylo4d constructor basically does the second thing 
above already if you supply both tip and node data, but it would store 
the first 3 rows in the @tip.data data frame and the last 2 rows in the 
@node.data data frame.

